answer:
**Clarify.** The wording is ambiguous: a `School` holds a list of student ids (`idList`). Assume you get a `List<School>` and need `Map<String, List<Integer>>` from school name to ids. Ask: can two schools share a name? If so, merge the lists (assume yes, merge).

**Classes**

```java
class Student {
    private final int id; private final String section; private final String house;
    Student(int id, String section, String house) { this.id = id; this.section = section; this.house = house; }
    int getId() { return id; }
}
class School {
    private final int id; private final String name; private final List<Integer> idList;
    School(int id, String name, List<Integer> idList) { this.id = id; this.name = name; this.idList = idList; }
    String getName() { return name; }
    List<Integer> getIdList() { return idList; }
}
```

**Loop version**

```java
Map<String, List<Integer>> bySchool = new HashMap<>();
for (School s : schools) {
    bySchool.computeIfAbsent(s.getName(), k -> new ArrayList<>())
            .addAll(s.getIdList());
}
```
`computeIfAbsent` creates the list on first use, so duplicates merge instead of overwriting. Time O(total ids), space O(total ids).

**Stream version**

```java
Map<String, List<Integer>> bySchool = schools.stream().collect(
    Collectors.toMap(School::getName, School::getIdList,
        (a, b) -> { List<Integer> m = new ArrayList<>(a); m.addAll(b); return m; }));
```
Without the merge function, `toMap` throws `IllegalStateException` on duplicate keys.

**If instead you are given students with a school id**, `groupingBy(Student::getSchoolId, mapping(Student::getId, toList()))` does it in one expression.

**Points to mention:** immutability (`final` fields, defensive copies of the list), `equals/hashCode` if objects are used as keys, null safety, `LinkedHashMap` or `TreeMap` if ordering matters.
